We always expect our researchers to be able to write code.

395
00:37:23,630 --> 00:37:31,680
我们也一直要求我们的工程师能够解决研究问题，所以这种跨学科的理解一直是核心理念。
We always expected our engineers to be able to solve research questions,
so this notion of understanding the different disciplines was always there.

396
00:37:32,206 --> 00:37:36,420
现在我认为，对于任何想要参与进来的人来说，机会都变得更大。
Now I think that the opportunity is even greater for anybody to actually open up.

397
00:37:36,900 --> 00:37:40,200
现在有更多的人能够开发应用并编写代码。
Now many more people can actually then develop applications and write code.

398
00:37:40,280 --> 00:37:57,124
更多的人可以成为特定领域的专家，并且有了 AI 的支持，他们可以通过运用正确的研究技能，
以过去难以想象的方式在科学研究中做出贡献。
Many more people can actually become experts in a particular domain, and with AI they can actually make a difference in scientific
research by actually applying the right research skills and in a way that was unthinkable in the past.

399
00:37:57,380 --> 00:37:57,620
没错。
Right.

400
00:37:57,720 --> 00:37:58,980
所以我们正处于「氛围编程」的时代。
So we're in the era of vibe coding.

401
00:37:59,180 --> 00:38:03,100
很快我们将进入「氛围药物研发」，到时候普通人也能设计出分子结构了。
Soon we go to vibe drug discovery, where just the average person is coming up with molecules.

402
00:38:03,540 --> 00:38:09,020
在洪水预测方面，这是否意味着我们终于能理解地球一直在发出的信号？
With flood prediction, is it finally understanding the signals of the planet that it's always been giving off?

403
00:38:10,190 --> 00:38:13,060
让我先从一个问题开始：为什么我们要研究洪水预测？
Let me start, actually, with a question of why did we look into flood prediction?

404
00:38:14,060 --> 00:38:19,000
我想我之前提到过，我们总是需要从「为什么」开始。
I think I mentioned earlier that we always need to start with the why.

405
00:38:19,470 --> 00:38:21,260
我们要做的事情会有什么影响？
What is the impact of what we're going to do?

406
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:31,880
在这个背景下，实际上，我负责所谓的危机韧性或危机响应工作已经有十年左右了。
In this context, actually, I've been overseeing what we call crisis resilience or crisis response for now a decade or so.

407
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:34,460
人们在遇到危机时会寻求 Google 的帮助。
People are coming to Google during crisis.

408
00:38:34,926 --> 00:38:44,676
所以我们一直在研究如何为他们提供的任何危机提供可操作的信息，
并实际上推出了一项名为 SOS alerts 的服务。
So we actually look into how can we actually give them with actionable information for any
crisis that they have and in fact launched it serves something that we call SOS alerts.

409
00:38:45,518 --> 00:38:51,000
但我发现的一点是，我们的帮助程度当然取决于我们能分享信息的质量。
But one thing I discovered is that we are as helpful, of course, that as the information that we can actually share.

410
00:38:51,000 --> 00:39:03,860
事实证明，最严重的自然灾害——那些每年造成数千人伤亡的洪水——之所以没能提供帮助，
是因为唯一能提供帮助的方法就是提供最好的信息。
and as it turns out, the most devastating natural disasters, floods, which are responsible for thousands of casualties every year,
were not helpful, because the only way to be helpful is actually to give —the best.

411
00:39:03,860 --> 00:39:08,040
也就是提供关于即将到来的洪水的预测，以便人们能够采取行动。
way to be helpful is to to give predictions about floods coming so that people can take action.

412
00:39:08,860 --> 00:39:19,760
十年前当我询问洪水专家时，关于我们如何提供预测，普遍的共识是这太难了。
Now, a decade ago when I asked people about the experts, flood experts, what can we do in order to provide prediction,
the general consensus was that this is too difficult.

413
00:39:20,590 --> 00:39:26,000
因为要获得有价值的预测，你需要高置信度预测，而且涉及太多变量等等。
Because in order to have a valuable prediction, you need to have high confidence prediction, too many variables and so forth.

414
00:39:26,862 --> 00:39:34,880
所以我们真正研究的是：虽然大家都说太难了，但这是一个值得去攻克的关键问题。
So what we really looked into is, well, everybody says it's too difficult, but it's an important problem to tackle.

415
00:39:34,880 --> 00:39:37,120
让我们看看我们能否取得进展。
Let's see if we can make progress about it.

416
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:41,060
我们最初从一些研究入手。
We started actually with some research.

417
00:39:43,300 --> 00:39:55,260
一旦有了足够的进展，我们在印度进行了一项试点，证明了在非常严苛的限制下，
我们确实可以做出一些合理的预测，这让我感到乐观。
Once we had enough progress, we actually ran a pilot in India and showed that we can actually do some reasonable prediction there
under very tight constraints, but enough to make me optimistic.

418
00:39:55,540 --> 00:40:03,780
事实上，早在大约八年前，我们就发表了一篇论文，提出了一个假设：
未来我们将能够在云端利用机器学习进行扩展。
In fact, we even published a paper back in almost eight years ago with a hypothesis
that in the future we're going to be able to scale with machine learning in the cloud.

419
00:40:04,462 --> 00:40:14,520
当时我不知道具体该如何实现，但已经有足够的迹象表明，
我们可以整合所有这些变量并尝试使用机器学习来实现目标。
I had no idea how we're going to do that, but there was enough indications that we can
actually take all these variables and try to use machine learning in order to do that.

420
00:40:15,246 --> 00:40:33,040
这确实需要多次在研究、实地测试、以及与学术界和政府建立合作伙伴之间循环迭代，
去寻找谁能提供数据供我们构建机器学习模型，并最终推动相关科学的发展。
It did require many cycles of going from research to trying it out in the field, to identifying what we can do to work with
partnerships, with academics, with governments to actually who collect the data that we could actually build machine learning models

421
00:40:33,040 --> 00:40:37,640
这确实需要多次在研究、实地测试、以及与学术界和政府建立合作伙伴之间循环迭代，
去寻找谁能提供数据供我们构建机器学习模型，并最终推动相关科学的发展。
on that, and eventually actually help drive the science for that.

422
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:42,000
我们在 Nature 上发表了一篇关于「全球水文模型」的论文。
We published a paper in Nature with what we call global hydrologic model.

423
00:40:43,580 --> 00:40:56,480
我们真正展示的是：我们可以建立从数据充足地区学
习洪水事件的模型，并将其应用于数据不足的地区。
And really what we showed is that we can actually build models that are learning from flood events in places that we have enough data
and then apply it also to places that we don't have as much data.

424
00:40:56,480 --> 00:41:09,990
原本被认为是一个「不可能的任务」，但现在我们不仅证明了可以推动相关科学，
而且同一团队还建立了一个系统，目前可在 150 个国家提供长达 7 天的预测，覆盖 20 亿人。
And what started as an impossible problem, quote-unquote, we actually now not only showed that we can drive the science,
but also the same team, we've built a system that now provides up to

425
00:41:09,990 --> 00:41:15,100
原本被认为是一个「不可能的任务」，但现在我们不仅证明了可以推动相关科学，
而且同一团队还建立了一个系统，目前可在 150 个国家提供长达 7 天的预测，覆盖 20 亿人。
seven days prediction in 150 countries, covering two billion people.

426
00:41:15,598 --> 00:41:22,880
所以它已经能救命了，因为它已经上线，可以通过
我们所谓的 Flathub 提供给救援人员和政府使用。
So it's already life-saving, because it's out there, it's available for responders, for governments, through what we call Flathub.

427
00:41:23,480 --> 00:41:32,100
这就是这种迭代的例子：从问题开始，采取研究步骤，转化为现实，然后提出下一个问题。
So, this is an example for this iterating, starting with a problem,
taking a research step, bringing it to reality, asking the next question.

428
00:41:32,500 --> 00:41:35,220
我喜欢把这称为「研究魔力循环」。
I like to call this the magic cycle of research.

429
00:41:35,918 --> 00:41:44,840
从某种意义上说，研究之所以比以往任何时候都更令人兴奋，
是因为这个魔力循环正在以前任何时候都更快地加速。
So, in a way, one reason why research is more exciting than ever is because this magic cycle is accelerating more than ever before.

430
00:41:45,358 --> 00:41:47,840
而且随着人工智能的使用，它在进一步加速。
And it's even further accelerating with the use of AI.

431
00:41:48,398 --> 00:41:51,140
我们实际上可以利用人工智能来让研究进展得更快。
We can actually use AI to actually drive the research faster.

432
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:55,524
然后我们可以利用人工智能来帮助将研究转化为现实。
we can then use AI to help bring the research to reality.

433
00:41:56,200 --> 00:41:57,820
这产生了巨大的影响。
And that has tremendous impact.

434
00:41:57,870 --> 00:41:59,720
在这种情况下，它实际上是拯救生命的。
In this case, it's actually life-saving.

435
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:01,720
这非常了不起，对吧？
That's extraordinary, right?

436
00:42:02,222 --> 00:42:07,420
想象一下，提前七天就收到洪水警报通知。
Imagine getting a notification seven days in advance of a flood, that there's going to be a flood.

437
00:42:07,886 --> 00:42:09,300
这真的能改变生活。
That's truly life-changing.

438
00:42:09,640 --> 00:42:21,740
而且，就在几个月前，尼日利亚政府和名为 GiveDirectly 的组织实际上利用我们的 Flood Hub
API 向村民发送资金，以便他们能提前撤离。
And, you know, just a few months ago, we had a government of Nigeria and organizations called GiveDirectly actually use an API to our
flood hub to actually send money to villagers so they can evacuate ahead of time.

439
00:42:22,670 --> 00:42:31,540
但这并不是故事的终点，因为在任何时刻，你都会进入某种程度的「这是我们能做的」，
然后是「这些是我们尚未解决的问题」。
But that's actually not the end of the story, because in any given moment, you're getting into a certain level of,
here's what we can do, now here are the problems that we haven't solved as yet.

440
00:42:32,110 --> 00:42:41,040
例如，在洪水背景下，我们遇到了另一个障碍，即
所有这些模型都只适用于我们所谓的河流和洪水。
For example, in the context of floods, we faced another wall, which is, all these models are true for what we call river and floods.

441
00:42:41,220 --> 00:42:43,000
这些是那些溢出河道的河流。
These are rivers that actually go overboard.

442
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:46,720
然而，一些破坏性的洪水实际上是山洪。
Now, some of the devastating floods are actually flash floods.

443
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:54,460
这些洪水来势突然，而且由于我们通常没有足够的数据来构建相关模型，它们往往难以预测。
These are floods that come, surprisingly, They come in a way that
typically we don't have enough data to actually build models for them.

444
00:42:55,982 --> 00:42:59,560
直到大约去年，这其实还是一个未解决的问题。
Until about last year, actually, this was kind of an unsolved problem.

445
00:43:00,560 --> 00:43:06,840
接下来是真正的创新，即我们发现了一个可以利用的切入点。
Here comes actually the next innovation, which was an observation that we could actually use.

446
00:43:07,580 --> 00:43:13,604
所有这些我们关心的洪水事件，至少在城市地区，通常都会出现在新闻中。
All these flood events that we care about, at least in urban areas, typically are reported in news.

447
00:43:15,440 --> 00:43:29,060
我们的想法是利用生成式人工智能，也就是使用 Gemini 来阅读 20 年的新闻，
识别新闻中报道的洪水事件，这些新闻通常也会描述发生的时间。
The idea was to actually use generative AI, to use GemIIni in order to read through news of 20 years,
identify events of floods reported in the news, which also described quite often when they happened.

448
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:36,324
这些都是多种语言的公开信息，我们基本上建立了一个事件数据库。
This is all public information in many languages, and essentially build a database of events.

449
00:43:36,942 --> 00:43:38,780
我们称这种技术为「地面源」。
We call this technique ground source.

450
00:43:39,118 --> 00:43:44,800
本质上是构建地面真值数据，以便我们能基于这些数据构建 AI 模型。
Essentially, build a ground truth data so that we can build AI models on them.

451
00:43:45,380 --> 00:43:56,500
因此，我们实际上利用生成式人工智能来建立并识别出 260 万起山洪事件，
然后我们就可以基于这些数据构建机器学习模型和 AI 模型。
So we used, actually, generative AI to build and to identify 2.6 million flash floods events,
which then we could actually build machine learning models, AI models based on them.

452
00:43:57,040 --> 00:44:02,860
并且我们将这种针对城市山洪的预测也提供到了 Flathub 上。
And we made this prediction for urban flash floods available as well on Flathub.

453
00:44:02,894 --> 00:44:17,260
所以，一个在仅仅一年前几乎无法解决的问题，现在通过结合生成式人工智能、
利用这些可用的数据以及建立与代理指标的联系，我们就能解决问题了。
So, a problem that was just practically unsolved just a year ago is now with the combination of generative AI and the observations
that you can actually use this data and have this connection to proxies, we can actually then solve problems.

454
00:44:17,860 --> 00:44:31,450
至于范式本身并不是什么……我很多年前和团队一起开发了 Google Trends，
我们发现一个非常有用的范式是如何利用 Google Trends——
Now, the paradigm itself is not something, you know, I developed Google Trends with my team many years ago,
and one paradigm that we've seen very useful is how to use Google trends, which essentially

455
00:44:31,450 --> 00:44:31,830
至于范式本身并不是什么……我很多年前和团队一起开发了 Google Trends，
我们发现一个非常有用的范式是如何利用 Google Trends——
people termed sometimes the database of intentions, to identify various phenomenas.

456
00:44:31,830 --> 00:44:37,476
人们有时称之为「意图数据库」——来识别各种现象。
people termed sometimes the database of intentions, to identify various phenomenas.

457
